WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.300
ASP.NET Core can configure CORS through middleware policies, endpoint routing, and attributes.

2
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:09.640
The right shape depends on the application.

3
00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:15.020
Some applications have one clearly defined browser frontend and one API surface.

4
00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:27.520
Others have public endpoints, internal operations endpoints, SignalR hubs, Razor Pages, controller APIs, Minimal API groups, and administrative routes that need different decisions.

5
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:31.160
Named policies are often the most reviewable pattern.

6
00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:39.180
A name such as ExamplePublicFrontendPolicy or ExampleAdminConsolePolicy communicates the relationship the policy is meant to support.

7
00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:40.880
The name should not be vague.

8
00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:48.660
Reviewers should be able to connect the policy to a real frontend, a real API surface, and a documented business requirement.

9
00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:55.500
A global CORS policy can be appropriate when the application truly has one consistent browser access model.

10
00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:02.880
But global policy can also overexpose unrelated endpoints when it is added only to solve one local issue.

11
00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:13.220
Before applying policy globally, check whether health endpoints, administrative APIs, file endpoints, SignalR hubs, and other surfaces should share that policy.

12
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:17.180
Endpoint-specific policy can reduce accidental trust.

13
00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:26.160
Minimal API groups, controller attributes, endpoint metadata, and route organization can make security intent visible where the endpoint is defined.

14
00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:33.540
This is especially useful when different clients need different origins, methods, headers, or credential behavior.

15
00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:38.440
The more varied the API surface, the more valuable explicit scoping becomes.

16
00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:41.760
Local development origins should not drift into production.

17
00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:52.180
Development hosts, temporary ports, preview URLs, and test origins can be useful while building, but production policy should come from approved configuration.

18
00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:58.120
Environment-specific settings should be reviewed so convenience does not become a permanent production permission.

19
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:01.520
The course does not need heavy code to make the point.

20
00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:05.620
The review question is what relationship the policy allows.

21
00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:14.920
Which frontend origin is named, which endpoints are in scope, which methods and headers are necessary, whether credentials are allowed, and who owns the decision.

22
00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:20.620
Configuration is safer when those answers are written into names, scope, and documentation.
